acrows=[['Accepted November service; unconditional payment right','$5,000 earned in November','Invoice Dec 1; collect Dec 15','Dr receivable / Cr revenue 5,000'],['November payroll earned','$1,800 November expense','Pay December 5','Dr wage expense / Cr payable 1,800'],['Insurance paid November 1; twelve months coverage','$100 November expense; $1,100 prepaid','$1,200 November payment','Dr insurance expense / Cr prepaid 100 at close'],['Customer advance for December service','No November revenue from this advance','$2,400 November receipt','Dr cash / Cr contract liability 2,400']]
add('N028','accrual-accounting','Accrual Accounting: Revenue, Expenses and Cash Timing','Accrual Accounting: Cash vs Earned Results','Compare earned revenue, incurred expenses and cash timing using one reconciled service-business example. Track receivables, prepayments and customer advances.','accrual accounting','Recognition overview, distinct from the cash forecast and later accrued-expense workflow.','Accrual accounting records revenue and expenses in the periods to which they belong under the accounting framework, rather than using cash dates alone. Separate the delivery, billing and settlement evidence before comparing profit with a bank balance.',[
('recognition','Identify what happened before choosing the date',p('For this introductory financial-reporting example, assume an accepted service gives an unconditional right to payment and all relevant recognition conditions are satisfied in November. The invoice is issued later. The invoice date by itself does not determine when revenue is earned. If payment depends on additional performance, a contract asset or another treatment may be relevant; do not automatically call every unbilled amount accounts receivable.')+p('Likewise, distinguish a service consumed, an asset purchased and an advance paid. A December payment for November work can settle an existing obligation. Insurance paid in advance can provide coverage over several periods. These distinctions organize the evidence; they are not an election of a permissible US tax method.')),
('dates','Use four transactions with separate accounting and cash dates',table('Original November 2026 accrual case; USD',['Event','November recognition','Cash / billing date','Illustrative posting'],acrows)+p('The insurance initially creates a $1,200 prepaid asset. One month of coverage is then consumed, leaving $1,100. The $2,400 advance is assumed refundable or deliverable under the contract and wholly unearned at November 30. It is not extra November sales merely because money arrived. No sales tax, bad-debt adjustment, depreciation or other expenses are included in this deliberately limited example.')),
('bridge','Reconcile November profit, cash and balances',p('Earned revenue is $5,000. Included expenses are $1,800 payroll plus $100 insurance, so accrual profit is <strong>$3,100</strong>. Operating receipts less payments in this case are $2,400 − $1,200 = $1,200. The $1,900 difference between profit and this operating cash movement is explained by $5,000 new receivable, $1,100 remaining prepaid, $1,800 unpaid wages and $2,400 customer advance: $3,100 − $5,000 − $1,100 + $1,800 + $2,400 = $1,200.')+p('Add a separate $10,000 owner cash investment at the beginning. Closing cash becomes $11,200, not $3,100. Assets are $11,200 cash + $5,000 receivable + $1,100 prepaid = $17,300. Liabilities are $1,800 wages + $2,400 advance = $4,200. Equity is $10,000 investment + $3,100 profit = $13,100. Thus $17,300 = $4,200 + $13,100.')+p('This is a financial-reporting illustration. A simple cash receipts/payments view of these transactions is not a complete tax-basis income computation. Loan principal, equipment purchases, deposits, constructive receipt and applicable tax rules can require additional analysis.')),
('settle','Carry the balances into December without counting twice',p('On December 15, collecting the $5,000 reduces the receivable and increases cash; it does not recognize the same service revenue again. Paying $1,800 wages settles the November payable. Consuming another month of insurance reduces the prepaid asset by another $100. Recognize the advance as earned only when the assumed December service conditions are satisfied, not automatically because the calendar changed.')+p('Preserve references from each settlement to its original transaction. Use '+link('accounts-receivable-process','the receivable workflow')+' for collection allocation and '+link('current-liabilities','the liability schedule')+' for unpaid obligations. A balanced entry can still contain the wrong period or classification.')),
('close','Use a cutoff review before reading the result',p('At month end, inspect accepted work, invoices issued before or after cutoff, services received but unbilled, unused prepayments and unfulfilled customer promises. Document the reporting basis and reviewer. Reconcile opening balances plus movements to closing schedules; do not replace missing evidence with an arbitrary journal to make profit look reasonable.')+p('The '+link('accounting-cycle','accounting cycle')+' connects adjustments to statements. '+link('bookkeeping-small-business','Bookkeeping for small business')+' covers the routine evidence collection, and the <a href="/calculators/cash-flow-forecast-template/">cash forecast</a> handles future payment timing. IRS Publication 538 is the separate official reference for tax accounting methods; this guide makes no blanket eligibility or change-of-method claim.'))
],['Event','Recognition','Cash / billing date','Posting'],acrows,['Transaction / evidence','Performance / consumption date','Invoice date','Payment date','Account / adjustment'],[('Does accrual accounting wait for the invoice?','Not necessarily. Recognition depends on the framework and transaction facts; an invoice date alone is insufficient.'),('Is an advance payment always revenue?','No. In this example it remains an obligation until the service is earned under the stated assumptions.'),('Does collecting a receivable increase revenue again?','No. It normally converts a previously recognized receivable into cash.'),('Does this determine which tax method I may use?','No. Tax eligibility and changes of method need a separate review of current official rules and your circumstances.')],[(ADJUST,'OpenStax: adjusting-entry framework'),('https://www.irs.gov/publications/p538','IRS Publication 538: separate tax accounting-method guidance')],'Workshop owner comparing delivery evidence, an invoice and calendar; titled Accrual Accounting.')

bookrows=[['Cash book before omitted bank fee','$9,750','8,000 opening + 6,200 receipts − 4,450 payments'],['Unrecorded bank fee','−$50','Post supported fee once'],['Adjusted cash book','$9,700','Agrees to adjusted bank evidence'],['Statement balance','$9,850','Statement at same month-end cutoff'],['Deposit in transit','+$250','Already in books; absent from bank'],['Outstanding payment','−$400','Already in books; absent from bank'],['Adjusted statement','$9,700','9,850 + 250 − 400']]
add('N029','bookkeeping-small-business','Bookkeeping for Small Business: A Monthly Record Workflow','Small Business Bookkeeping: Monthly Workflow','Organize transaction evidence, reconcile cash and customer balances, review adjustments and close the month with an original bank-reconciliation example.','bookkeeping for small business','Recordkeeping and close workflow, not software recommendations or accounting-method selection.','Small-business bookkeeping turns supported transactions into consistent records and reconciled balances. A useful monthly routine collects evidence, posts transactions once, resolves differences and preserves a reviewed closing package.',[
('evidence','Build the record around evidence rather than the bank feed',p('Keep an identifiable link between each posting and the underlying sale, supplier bill, receipt, payroll record or agreement. The IRS describes supporting business documents as the evidence behind entries. Its tax-record guidance is not a substitute for your financial-reporting policy, but it reinforces why a bank description alone may not explain what a transaction represents.')+p('Assign a unique reference, reporting date, amount, currency, counterparty and account. Separate business and personal activity appropriately. A bank feed entry can match an invoice payment already recorded; importing it as another sale would duplicate income. Keep unidentified items in a visible investigation list rather than assigning convenient categories.')),
('rhythm','Separate daily capture from the monthly close',table('A practical sequence with observable completion evidence',['Frequency','Work','Evidence of completion'],[['Daily / weekly','Collect and match documents; post invoices and payments','Unique references; no unexplained duplicates'],['Weekly','Review customer and supplier open items','Detail reconciles to control accounts'],['Month end','Reconcile cash, cards, loans and relevant stock','Dated schedules with differences explained'],['Month end','Review accruals, prepayments and asset schedules','Approved adjustments linked to evidence'],['Close','Review reports and preserve the version','Reviewer, unresolved issues and next actions']])+p('Frequency depends on transaction volume and commitments, not a universal deadline invented by this guide. A business with payroll this week may need cash review before the formal month-end close. The formal close should use a consistent cutoff across reports.')),
('cash','Reconcile a bank difference without inventing a plug',table('Original cash-reconciliation case, USD',['Record / adjustment','Amount','Reason'],bookrows)+p('The $50 fee is missing from the books, so it needs a supported book entry. The $250 deposit and $400 outstanding payment are already recorded in the books; adding or subtracting them there again would duplicate their effects. Their role is to explain the statement timing. Both adjusted views reach <strong>$9,700</strong>.')+p('An equal result does not validate every payment. Verify the recipient, amount and business purpose. Investigate old reconciling items and retain evidence of later clearing. Do not force unexplained differences into fees or owner equity.')),
('balances','Reconcile detail before accepting the report totals',p('In a separate customer subledger example, $3,000 opening receivables + $6,000 invoices − $5,200 allocated collections = $3,800 closing gross receivables. If the GL says $3,600, identify the $200 difference before treating the report as reconciled. Check credits, manual journals, duplicate invoices and unapplied receipts. This is independent of the bank example’s total receipts.')+p('Use '+link('accounts-receivable-process','AR records')+' and '+link('accounts-payable-process','AP records')+' for their respective workflows. Match asset cost and accumulated depreciation to an asset register. Check prepayment periods and accrued obligations. An account can reconcile mathematically while its valuation or classification still needs review.')),
('close','Preserve the close and the unresolved-action list',p('Review unusual balances and changes against actual operations. Retain the trial balance, reconciliations, adjustment support and reports with their cutoff date. Assign an owner and deadline to each remaining issue. A lock date is useful only when corrections are still made through a controlled, documented process rather than silently changing prior reports.')+p('The '+link('general-ledger-template','general ledger template')+' supplies posting references and account-level balances; the '+link('accounting-cycle','accounting cycle')+' explains adjustments and closing. The '+link('how-to-analyze-financial-statements','statement-analysis guide')+' addresses interpretation after the records are prepared. Check the actual retention requirements for each document type; this article does not assert a single period for every business record.'))
],['Record / adjustment','Amount','Reason'],bookrows,['Account / cutoff','Book balance','External evidence','Adjustment / reference','Reviewed balance / owner'],[('Is the bank feed the whole bookkeeping system?','No. It does not by itself explain unpaid invoices, noncash adjustments or the purpose and period of every transaction.'),('Should deposits in transit be entered again?','Not if already recorded correctly. Use them to explain timing between books and statement.'),('Can I close with an unexplained difference?','Record it as unresolved and investigate; do not conceal it with an unsupported balancing entry.'),('Does this guide recommend accounting software?','No. It defines the evidence and reconciliation workflow independently of a product.')],[(RECORDS,'IRS: supporting business records'),('https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/how-long-should-i-keep-records','IRS: record-specific retention considerations')],'Monthly receipts, bank evidence and bookkeeping trays; titled Small Business Bookkeeping.')

cyclerows=[['Cash','13,100','13,100','Debit'],['Accounts receivable','1,500','1,500','Debit'],['Unused supplies','900','600','Debit'],['Rent expense','600','600','Debit'],['Supplies expense','0','300','Debit'],['Wages expense','0','700','Debit'],['Accounts payable','900','900','Credit'],['Wages payable','0','700','Credit'],['Customer advance liability','1,200','800','Credit'],['Owner capital','10,000','10,000','Credit'],['Service revenue','4,000','4,400','Credit'],['Debit / credit totals','16,100 each','16,800 each','Balanced']]
add('N030','accounting-cycle','Accounting Cycle: From Transactions to Closed Statements','Accounting Cycle: A Reconciled Worked Example','Trace one service-business month through journal entries, ledger balances, adjustments, financial statements and closing with checked trial-balance totals.','accounting cycle','The recording-to-close sequence, separate from statement interpretation and ledger-template structure.','The accounting cycle connects transaction evidence to journal entries, ledger accounts, adjustments, statements and closing. Keep the unadjusted, adjusted and post-closing balances separate so each stage has a traceable purpose.',[
('record','Record six supported events before adjusting',p('This fictional sole-proprietor service business begins with zero balances. The owner invests $10,000 cash. It buys $900 supplies on account, earns and invoices $4,000 of accepted services, collects $2,500 of that receivable, pays $600 rent for the month, and receives $1,200 for future services. No tax, depreciation, inventory for resale or owner drawings are included.')+p('Journal entries preserve the two-sided effect of each event. For example, supplies purchased on account debit Supplies and credit Accounts Payable by $900. Collecting $2,500 debits Cash and credits Accounts Receivable; it does not record another sale. Post each journal reference into the appropriate ledger accounts.')),
('adjust','Keep the adjustment evidence distinct',p('At month end, a supported count shows $600 supplies remain, so $300 has been used. Employees have earned $700 unpaid wages. Of the $1,200 advance, $400 is now earned under the case’s service-recognition assumptions. Record three adjustments: Dr Supplies Expense / Cr Supplies $300; Dr Wages Expense / Cr Wages Payable $700; Dr Customer Advance Liability / Cr Service Revenue $400.')+p('These entries change the period’s result and balances without creating a cash transaction at the adjustment date. Preserve the count, payroll cutoff and delivery evidence. Adjusting to a desired profit number is not a valid substitute for that support.')),
('trial','Compare unadjusted and adjusted trial balances',table('Original trial-balance comparison, USD',['Account','Unadjusted balance','Adjusted balance','Normal side'],cyclerows)+p('The unadjusted trial balance totals $16,100 on each side. After adjustments, both sides total <strong>$16,800</strong>. Equal totals show posting equality, not proof that the right accounts and periods were used. An omitted event or a wrong but balanced entry can escape this test.')),
('statements','Prepare statements from the adjusted balances',p('Recognized revenue is $4,400. Expenses are $600 rent + $300 supplies + $700 wages = $1,600, leaving $2,800 profit. Closing equity before any drawings is $10,000 investment + $2,800 profit = $12,800. Assets are $13,100 cash + $1,500 receivables + $600 supplies = $15,200. Liabilities are $900 AP + $700 wages + $800 unearned advance = $2,400. The balance sheet reconciles: $15,200 = $2,400 + $12,800.')+p('Cash increased $13,100 from zero, which differs from profit because owner funding, collections, credit purchases and the customer advance have different effects. The cash-flow statement and reporting framework require their own classification; do not copy the profit subtotal into a cash balance.')),
('closing','Close temporary accounts without deleting history',p('For this illustrative sole-proprietor closing convention, debit Revenue $4,400 and credit Income Summary. Debit Income Summary $1,600 and credit the three expense accounts. Transfer the remaining $2,800 credit from Income Summary to Owner Capital. Revenue, expenses and Income Summary then have zero balances; capital is $12,800. There are no drawings to close in this case.')+p('The post-closing trial balance contains $15,200 debit assets and $15,200 credit liabilities/equity. Real systems may automate closing or use different equity accounts; do not post these manual entries on top of an automated close. Preserve the transaction history and the reports used for approval.')),
('review','Use each report for its own question',p('The journal explains what was recorded; '+link('general-ledger-template','the ledger')+' groups postings by account. The trial balance checks aggregate equality; the statements report the resulting position and performance. '+link('bookkeeping-small-business','The bookkeeping routine')+' gathers evidence and reconciles detail. '+link('accrual-accounting','Accrual accounting')+' explains why dates can differ.')+p('Keep the prior period’s closing balances as the next period’s opening balances and record correction references. Use '+link('how-to-analyze-financial-statements','statement analysis')+' after the close to interpret the result, rather than treating interpretation as another posting step.'))
],['Account','Unadjusted','Adjusted','Side'],cyclerows,['Step / reference','Source evidence','Debit account / amount','Credit account / amount','Reconciliation / reviewer'],[('Why prepare more than one trial balance?','They show distinct stages: before adjustments, after adjustments and after temporary accounts are closed.'),('Does a balanced trial balance prove correctness?','No. Omissions, wrong classification and incorrect timing can still leave equal totals.'),('Does closing delete old transactions?','No. It resets temporary balances under the selected closing convention while preserving history.'),('Should I manually close software that already closes automatically?','Do not duplicate automated closing. Follow the actual system and reporting policy.')],[(ADJUST,'OpenStax: adjustment process and trial-balance stages'),(CLOSE,'OpenStax: closing-entry sequence')],'Accountant organizing document stages at an office pinboard; titled The Accounting Cycle.')

ledgerrows=[['Nov 1','J01','Owner contribution','5,000','0','5,000 Dr'],['Nov 3','J02','Equipment purchased','0','1,800','3,200 Dr'],['Nov 12','J04','Customer invoice collection','1,600','0','4,800 Dr'],['Nov 18','J05','Period expense paid','0','250','4,550 Dr']]
add('N031','general-ledger-template','General Ledger Template: Posting References and Reconciliation','General Ledger Template: Reconciled Example','Use an editable account-level ledger with posting references, debit and credit columns, a checked cash example and a matching trial-balance reconciliation.','general ledger template','Account-level posting and reconciliation record; not an automatic accounting system or journal-entry policy.','A general ledger groups posted transactions by account. A useful template records the date, journal reference, explanation, debit, credit and running balance, then ties the closing account to independent evidence and the trial balance.',[
('fields','Use one account identity and one balance convention',p('The downloadable HTML template is editable and printable. It includes a completed Cash ledger and a blank working record with evidence and reviewer fields. It does not automatically calculate, post journals, create a trial balance or save edits. This limitation is deliberate: users must verify each result rather than assuming a blank manual template is an accounting application.')+p('Set the account name/number, period, currency, opening balance and normal side. For a debit-balance account, running balance equals opening balance + debits − credits. A credit-balance account can instead use opening credit balance + credits − debits. Label the sign convention. Debit does not universally mean money received, and credit does not universally mean a cash payment.')),
('journal','Retain the journal behind the account detail',table('Original supporting journal; each entry balances, USD',['Ref','Event','Debit','Credit'],[['J01','Owner contribution','Cash 5,000','Owner capital 5,000'],['J02','Equipment bought for cash','Equipment 1,800','Cash 1,800'],['J03','Accepted service invoiced','Receivable 2,200','Revenue 2,200'],['J04','Part collection of J03','Cash 1,600','Receivable 1,600'],['J05','Period service expense paid','Expense 250','Cash 250']])+p('J03 does not appear in the Cash account because it creates a receivable rather than a cash receipt. It must still appear in the receivable and revenue ledgers. A cash-only record cannot replace a complete general ledger. Keep document references separate from journal references when the two identify different evidence.')),
('cash','Check the completed Cash account',table('Original account 100 Cash; opening zero; USD debit-balance convention',['Date','Journal ref','Description','Debit','Credit','Running balance'],ledgerrows)+p('Debits total $6,600 and credits $2,050. The closing Cash debit balance is <strong>$4,550</strong>. Do not add a later bank-feed match as another $1,600 collection. Trace the existing receipt to J04 and the related customer invoice.')+p('For a nonzero opening balance, retain evidence that it equals the prior period’s reviewed closing account. An unexplained opening balance can make every subsequent row appear orderly while the account remains wrong.')),
('trial','Reconcile the other ledgers and the trial balance',table('Closing balances from the same five entries, USD',['Account','Debit','Credit'],[['Cash','4,550','0'],['Receivables','600','0'],['Equipment','1,800','0'],['Expense','250','0'],['Owner capital','0','5,000'],['Revenue','0','2,200'],['Total','7,200','7,200']])+p('The receivable closes at $2,200 − $1,600 = $600. Revenue less the included expense is $1,950. Assets are $4,550 + $600 + $1,800 = $6,950; equity is $5,000 + $1,950 = $6,950, with no liabilities in this case. Equal trial-balance totals still require evidence and classification review.')+p('Match Cash to a bank reconciliation, the receivable control to customer detail and Equipment to the asset register. A bank statement is not evidence for every GL account. Document and resolve differences rather than making unsupported entries to match a total.')),
('use','Maintain references and corrections as the ledger grows',p('Use unique journal references, preserve the original entry and show the approved correcting or reversing entry. Keep dates and account names consistent. A sorted display is not an audit trail if someone can silently replace prior amounts. In a live system, access control, backups and period locks need a separate design.')+p('Use '+link('bookkeeping-small-business','the bookkeeping workflow')+' for the monthly evidence package and '+link('accounting-cycle','the accounting cycle')+' for adjusting and closing. '+link('accounts-receivable-process','Customer detail')+' and '+link('accounts-payable-process','supplier detail')+' support their respective control accounts. This template does not choose the recognition policy for every transaction.'))
],['Date','Journal reference','Description','Debit','Credit','Running balance'],ledgerrows,['Date / journal ref','Description / evidence','Debit','Credit','Balance / normal side'],[('Is the ledger the same as the journal?','No. The journal records entries in sequence; the ledger groups their postings by account.'),('Does this template calculate automatically?','No. It is an editable printable HTML record. Verify running balances and totals independently.'),('Can I reconcile every account to the bank?','No. Different accounts require different evidence, such as customer detail or an asset register.'),('Does a debit always mean a cash receipt?','No. Debit and credit effects depend on the account and transaction.')],[(POST,'OpenStax: journal entries and account posting'),(RECORDS,'IRS: documents supporting business books')],'Ruled ledger book, posting tabs and document scanner; titled General Ledger Template.')
methodrows=[['Straight line','$2,400','$11,600','12,000 depreciable base / 5 years'],['Units of production','$3,000','$11,000','12,000 / 60,000 units × 15,000 units'],['Double declining balance','$5,600','$8,400','40% × 14,000 opening carrying amount']]
add('N032','depreciation-methods','Depreciation Methods: Asset Cost, Useful Life and Profit Impact','Depreciation Methods: Cost and Profit Impact','Compare straight-line, units-of-production and declining-balance depreciation using asset cost, residual value and life assumptions, with checked effects.','depreciation','Method selection and reporting effects; detailed straight-line schedule and accumulated-balance rollforward have separate owners.','Depreciation allocates a depreciable asset’s cost over its estimated useful life under the reporting policy. Choose a method that reflects the expected consumption pattern; depreciation is not a direct estimate of market value or a cash reserve for replacement.',[
('basis','Define the asset and depreciable amount first',p('Start with supported capitalized cost, the date the asset is available for its intended use, useful life, residual value and the relevant consumption pattern. Distinguish repairs and period expenses from costs that meet capitalization requirements. Do not classify an item as an asset only because you prefer a smaller current expense.')+p('The original example uses equipment with $14,000 capitalized cost, $2,000 estimated residual value and a five-year useful life. The depreciable amount is $12,000. The example assumes a full first year available for use, no impairment, revaluation, disposal or estimate change, and USD financial-reporting amounts. It is not a tax-depreciation election or industry life recommendation.')),
('compare','Compare methods on exactly the same starting asset',table('Original first-year comparison; three alternative policies, not cumulative charges',['Method','Year-one allocation','Closing carrying amount','Calculation'],methodrows)+p('Straight line allocates $2,400 in each full year under unchanged estimates. Units of production uses a selected total life output of 60,000 units and first-year output of 15,000: $12,000 / 60,000 × 15,000 = $3,000. The 40% double-declining rate is twice the five-year straight-line rate and applies to opening carrying amount. Residual value is enforced as a floor; it is not subtracted again before applying that rate.')+p('Do not add the three charges. They are alternative methods for the same asset. Their different first-year amounts reflect different assumed consumption patterns, not three separate costs to post.')),
('pattern','Test whether the method fits the consumption evidence',p('A reasonably even time-based consumption pattern may support straight line. A measurable usage relationship may support units of production when output records and lifetime capacity estimates are credible. A declining pattern requires support for higher earlier consumption; choosing it only to lower reported profit is not a method-selection argument.')+p('For the declining-balance illustration, full-year charges are $5,600, $3,360, $2,016, $1,024 and $0 after capping at the $2,000 residual. Total allocation is $12,000. The fourth year’s uncapped 40% amount would exceed the remaining depreciable amount, so it is limited. Another documented policy might switch methods; this schedule assumes no switch.')),
('effects','Separate expense, inventory, cash and carrying amount',p('If revenue before depreciation is $30,000 and other included period expenses are $20,000, the pre-depreciation result is $10,000. Treating all depreciation as a period expense in this simplified service case gives results of $7,600, $7,000 or $4,400 under the three alternatives. The asset acquisition cash payment is the same $14,000 in all three cases; the allocation itself does not pay another $2,400, $3,000 or $5,600.')+p('In production settings, depreciation can enter manufacturing overhead and inventory before reaching cost of goods sold. Do not force every asset’s depreciation into a separate current OPEX line. Reconcile the actual capitalization and expense policy. The '+link('how-to-calculate-cost-of-goods-sold','COGS guide')+' retains its cost boundary.')),
('review','Maintain estimates and keep the specialist schedules separate',p('Document the selected method, estimate evidence, reviewer and effective date. Review a change in expected life, residual amount or use under the relevant accounting framework; do not erase prior history or call every revision an error. Impairment and disposal are different questions and can change the carrying amount outside ordinary depreciation.')+p('Use '+link('straight-line-depreciation','the straight-line tutorial')+' for partial periods and a complete dated schedule. Use '+link('accumulated-depreciation','the accumulated-depreciation rollforward')+' for balances and asset removals. The '+link('accounting-cycle','accounting cycle')+' shows where supported adjustments enter statements. Tax deductions, bonus depreciation and section 179 require separate current tax guidance and are outside this financial-reporting illustration.'))
],['Method','Year-one allocation','Carrying amount','Calculation'],methodrows,['Asset / in-use date','Supported cost / residual','Life / output assumption','Method / period allocation','Reviewer / reconciliation'],[('Does depreciation measure market value?','No. It allocates cost under a reporting policy. Market valuation and impairment are separate analyses.'),('Can I post all three methods together?','No. The table compares alternatives for one asset.'),('Does depreciation create replacement cash?','No. The noncash allocation does not fund a bank account.'),('Are these useful lives or tax rates recommended for my business?','No. They are fictional assumptions; actual reporting and tax treatment need their own evidence.')],[(DEP,'OpenStax: cost-allocation methods and assumptions')],'Engineer inspecting workshop equipment; titled Depreciation Methods.')

accrows=[['Opening gross asset cost','50,000'],['Additions at supported cost','10,000'],['Disposed asset cost removed','−12,000'],['Closing gross asset cost','48,000'],['Opening accumulated depreciation','18,000 credit'],['Current period depreciation','6,000 credit movement'],['Accumulated depreciation removed on disposal','8,400 debit movement'],['Closing accumulated depreciation','15,600 credit'],['Closing carrying amount','32,400']]
add('N033','accumulated-depreciation','Accumulated Depreciation: Balance-Sheet Rollforward and Disposal','Accumulated Depreciation: Balance Rollforward','Reconcile asset cost, current depreciation and disposal removals to closing accumulated depreciation and carrying amount using a checked original example.','accumulated depreciation','Contra-asset balance and disposal reconciliation, distinct from method selection and straight-line calculation.','Accumulated depreciation is the cumulative depreciation recorded against an asset or asset group that remains on the books. Reconcile opening balance, current allocations and removals separately from gross asset cost to explain the closing carrying amount.',[
('distinction','Distinguish the period allocation from the balance',p('Depreciation expense or capitalized depreciation is a current-period movement. Accumulated depreciation is a balance at a date. Gross cost and accumulated depreciation generally remain separate in a cost-model asset register; their difference supports carrying amount, subject to other relevant adjustments. Accumulated depreciation is not a liability owed to a supplier.')+p('This fictional rollforward assumes a cost model, no impairment, revaluation, foreign exchange, transfers or estimate changes. The stated $6,000 allocation has all been treated as period expense for simplicity and includes depreciation through the disposal date. A manufacturing asset register can require a separate inventory-cost reconciliation.')),
('rollforward','Reconcile gross cost and accumulated depreciation independently',table('Original asset-group rollforward, USD',['Line','Amount / balance'],accrows)+p('Gross cost closes at $50,000 + $10,000 − $12,000 = $48,000. Accumulated depreciation closes at $18,000 + $6,000 − $8,400 = <strong>$15,600</strong>. The resulting carrying amount is $48,000 − $15,600 = $32,400. Adding the $6,000 again to closing accumulated depreciation would duplicate the period allocation.')+p('The opening carrying amount was $32,000. Add $10,000 cost, subtract $6,000 depreciation and subtract the disposed asset’s $3,600 carrying amount: $32,000 + $10,000 − $6,000 − $3,600 = $32,400. The gross and net reconciliations agree.')),
('disposal','Remove both the disposed cost and its accumulated balance',p('The disposed asset has $12,000 cost and $8,400 accumulated depreciation at sale, leaving $3,600 carrying amount. Suppose it sells for $4,000 with no selling costs. The illustrative entry debits Cash $4,000 and Accumulated Depreciation $8,400, credits Asset Cost $12,000 and Gain on Disposal $400. Debits and credits both total $12,400.')+p('The $4,000 receipt is sale proceeds, not all gain. The $8,400 debit removes the disposed asset’s contra balance; it is not negative current depreciation. If only cost were removed, the remaining register would retain depreciation belonging to an asset no longer held. If the period allocation already runs through the disposal date, do not record it a second time.')),
('controls','Tie the rollforward to individual asset evidence',p('Assign a durable asset ID to cost, in-use date, method, life, residual value, accumulated balance and disposal record. Reconcile opening IDs plus additions less removals to the closing register. Check fully depreciated assets still used: they may remain in the register until disposal, with cost offset by accumulated depreciation under the policy.')+p('A total can reconcile while a disposed asset remains wrongly assigned to an active location. Verify ownership, use and disposal approvals as well as arithmetic. Do not infer a gain, loss or impairment solely from a low carrying amount. Those conclusions require separate transaction and valuation evidence.')),
('next','Use a balance reconciliation for the right decision',p('Use '+link('depreciation-methods','the methods overview')+' when evaluating the consumption pattern. Use '+link('straight-line-depreciation','the straight-line schedule')+' to compute a method-specific time allocation. The '+link('general-ledger-template','ledger template')+' supports posting references, while the '+link('accounting-cycle','close workflow')+' connects the register to statements.')+p('This article explains a financial-reporting balance. Tax basis, accumulated tax depreciation and proceeds treatment may differ and are not determined here. Preserve both schedules when required rather than overwriting financial-statement balances with tax amounts.'))
],['Line','Amount / balance'],accrows,['Asset ID / evidence','Opening cost / accumulated','Additions / allocation','Disposal cost / accumulated removed','Closing cost / accumulated / net'],[('Is accumulated depreciation an expense for this month?','No. It is a cumulative balance. Current-period depreciation is one movement in that balance.'),('Does a disposal remove only the original cost?','No. Remove the associated accumulated depreciation as well, after updating through the disposal date as required.'),('Is the sale receipt the gain?','No. Compare net proceeds with the relevant carrying amount.'),('Must a fully depreciated asset disappear from the register?','Not merely because it is fully depreciated; confirm the actual disposal and applicable policy.')],[(DISPOSE,'OpenStax: disposal of long-term assets'),('https://www.intacct.com/ia/docs/en_US/help_action/FixedAssets/Disposal/full-disposal-examples.htm','Sage Intacct: disposal and net-book-value examples')],'Asset acquisition and disposal folders beside workshop machines; titled Accumulated Depreciation.')

slrows=[['2027 Apr–Dec','9','1,800','1,800','12,200'],['2028','12','2,400','4,200','9,800'],['2029','12','2,400','6,600','7,400'],['2030','12','2,400','9,000','5,000'],['2031','12','2,400','11,400','2,600'],['2032 Jan–Mar','3','600','12,000','2,000'],['Total','60','12,000','12,000 closing','2,000 residual']]
add('N034','straight-line-depreciation','Straight-Line Depreciation: Formula, Partial Periods and Schedule','Straight-Line Depreciation: Worked Schedule','Calculate straight-line depreciation with supported cost, residual value, useful life and partial periods. Reconcile a complete editable sixty-month schedule.','straight line method of depreciation','Single-method formula and dated schedule; parent methods comparison and accumulated-balance disposal case stay separate.','Straight-line depreciation allocates the depreciable amount evenly across the selected time-based useful life. Annual allocation equals cost minus residual value, divided by life in years; a partial-period schedule must use an explicit start date and time convention.',[
('inputs','Define cost, life, residual and in-use date',p('For this independent service-asset case, capitalized cost is $14,000, residual value $2,000 and useful life five years. The asset is available for intended use on April 1, 2027. The example adopts a whole-month convention beginning that date and assumes no impairment, disposal, method switch or estimate change. These are teaching assumptions, not mandatory asset lives or a universal partial-month rule.')+p('The purchase invoice can precede readiness for use. Keep evidence of the in-use date and explain any capitalization policy. Tax conventions can differ from this financial-reporting month count; do not automatically apply a tax half-year or mid-quarter convention to this schedule.')),
('formula','Compute the annual and monthly amounts once',p('Depreciable amount = $14,000 − $2,000 = $12,000. Annual straight-line depreciation = $12,000 / 5 = <strong>$2,400</strong>. Monthly amount = $2,400 / 12 = $200. Equivalently, $12,000 / 60 months = $200 per month. The full-year rate is 20% of the depreciable amount, not 20% of cost without considering residual value.')+p('April through December includes nine months under the stated convention, so 2027 allocation is $200 × 9 = $1,800. Nine months is not a universal rule for every April purchase; it follows this exact in-use date and policy.')),
('schedule','Reconcile the complete calendar-year schedule',table('Original sixty-month straight-line schedule; amounts in USD',['Calendar period','Months','Period allocation','Closing accumulated','Closing carrying amount'],slrows)+p('The six calendar rows cover only five years of useful life: 9 + 12 + 12 + 12 + 12 + 3 = 60 months. Four full intervening calendar years allocate $9,600. The first $1,800 and final $600 complete the $12,000 depreciable amount. Closing carrying amount stops at the $2,000 residual; do not charge another full $2,400 in 2032.')+'<figure style="margin:24px 0"><img style="max-width:100%;height:auto" src="/images/diagrams/day4-straight-line.svg" width="960" height="460" loading="lazy" alt="Carrying amount falls from 14,000 dollars at April 2027 to 12,200, 9,800, 7,400, 5,000, 2,600 and finally 2,000 in March 2032."><figcaption>Exact carrying amounts from the original dated schedule.</figcaption></figure>'),
('posting','Post the allocation without inventing another cash payment',p('If the first nine months are all period expense in this service example, debit Depreciation Expense and credit Accumulated Depreciation by $1,800 for 2027. The original asset cost remains $14,000, accumulated depreciation becomes $1,800 and carrying amount is $12,200. No cash leaves the bank merely because that entry is posted.')+p('Production assets may contribute depreciation to inventory costs instead. The example does not prescribe that classification for every business. Keep acquisition cash, capitalization, current allocation and eventual expense recognition traceable rather than automatically treating them as one payment.')),
('revision','Handle changes and rounding with a documented policy',p('Reassess estimates when supported facts change. A change in remaining life or residual value may change future allocation; do not silently rewrite old months or automatically treat an estimate revision as an error. An actual disposal or impairment also requires its own analysis. Use the applicable framework and approval process.')+p('For a monthly amount that is not exact to cents, retain calculation precision and reconcile the final allocation to the intended residual. Do not round every input repeatedly and then allow the schedule to drift below its floor. This worksheet is manual and does not enforce that check automatically.')),
('links','Keep the method tutorial separate from the balance rollforward',p('The '+link('depreciation-methods','depreciation overview')+' compares time, usage and declining patterns. The '+link('accumulated-depreciation','accumulated-depreciation guide')+' reconciles additions, current allocations and removals. '+link('bookkeeping-small-business','The bookkeeping close')+' provides the recurring review routine.')+p('Record your cost evidence, readiness date, life, residual, convention and reviewer in the downloadable schedule. If any is unknown, the result is provisional; a precise formula cannot supply missing business evidence.'))
],['Period','Months','Allocation','Closing accumulated','Closing carrying amount'],slrows,['Period / in-use evidence','Months / convention','Cost / residual / life','Allocation / accumulated','Carrying amount / review'],[('Why does a five-year asset appear in six calendar years?','The first and last calendar years are partial. The schedule still contains exactly sixty months.'),('Does straight line use cost without residual value?','The depreciable amount is cost less the supported residual estimate.'),('Is purchase date always the depreciation start date?','Not necessarily. Check readiness for intended use and the applicable reporting policy.'),('Does this calculate a tax deduction?','No. It is a financial-reporting illustration with a stated time convention.')],[(DEP,'OpenStax: straight-line cost allocation'),('https://oer.hax.psu.edu/lnm105/sites/acctg211/07_lesson/06_page.html','Penn State: partial-year illustration; conventions must be specified')],'Camera equipment and a time schedule; titled Straight-Line Depreciation.')

FASB='https://storage.fasb.org/ASU%202025-05.pdf'
agerows=[['Not past due','50,000','1%','500'],['1–30 days past due','20,000','4%','800'],['Over 30 days past due','10,000','15%','1,500'],['Total gross receivables','80,000','Illustrative rates only','2,800 required closing allowance']]
allowroll=[['Opening allowance credit','1,200'],['Write-offs debited to allowance','−700'],['Reinstatement on recovery','+200'],['Credit balance before new provision','700'],['Required closing allowance','2,800'],['Current provision credit needed','2,100']]
add('N035','allowance-doubtful-accounts','Allowance for Doubtful Accounts: Estimate and Reconcile','Allowance for Doubtful Accounts: Example','Build an illustrative receivables aging estimate and reconcile opening allowance, write-offs, recoveries and the provision needed for the closing balance.','allowance for doubtful accounts','Allowance estimation and rollforward under an explicit US GAAP educational scope; not a tax deduction or assurance of CECL compliance.','An allowance for doubtful accounts reduces gross receivables to a supported carrying amount for expected credit losses. Estimate the required closing balance, then reconcile the existing allowance before calculating the provision; the required balance is not automatically this period’s expense.',[
('framework','State the reporting framework and current policy choices',p('This educational example uses US GAAP credit-loss terminology for ordinary trade receivables. It is not a complete ASC 326 model or a substitute for entity-specific accounting review. Aging percentages are fictional assumptions, not prescribed rates. Assess relevant experience, current conditions, customer-specific evidence and the applicable forecasting requirements.')+p('FASB ASU 2025-05 introduced optional simplifications for in-scope current receivables and contract assets. A current-conditions practical expedient and, for qualifying nonpublic entities using it, a subsequent-collections policy election require their own scope and disclosure assessment. The example below assumes neither election. Do not infer that every entity must use the same forecast or subsequent-cash treatment.')),
('aging','Build the closing estimate from a defined receivables population',table('Original November 30 aging illustration; USD, due-date buckets',['Bucket','Gross balance','Assumed loss rate','Estimated allowance'],agerows)+p('The three amounts reconcile to $80,000 gross AR. The required closing allowance is $500 + $800 + $1,500 = <strong>$2,800</strong>, giving a $77,200 net carrying amount. Review whether similar risk characteristics support the grouping and whether any balance needs separate assessment. Avoid applying both a pooled rate and a second specific allowance to the same exposure without reconciling the overlap.')+p('A bucket percentage must have a defensible basis and an update process in a real model. The arithmetic alone does not establish a compliant loss estimate. Preserve how credits, disputes, write-offs and subsequent evidence affect the population and rates.')),
('rollforward','Reconcile the existing allowance before recording the provision',table('Original allowance movement before and after estimation, USD credit-positive convention',['Movement / target','Amount'],allowroll)+p('Opening credit $1,200 − $700 write-offs + $200 recovery reinstatement leaves $700 credit before the new provision. To reach $2,800, record a $2,100 provision: Dr Credit Loss / Bad Debt Expense, Cr Allowance. Recording the entire $2,800 as new expense would overstate this example’s provision by $700.')+p('If the pre-adjustment allowance instead had a $300 debit balance, reaching $2,800 credit would require a $3,100 credit provision. Keep debit and credit signs visible; subtracting an unsigned balance can produce the wrong answer.')),
('gross','Reconcile gross and net AR as well as the allowance',p('For the same fictional period, opening gross AR is $60,000. Add $40,000 credit sales, deduct $19,300 ordinary collections and $700 write-offs, reinstate $200 recovered receivable and collect that $200. Closing gross AR is $80,000. Total collections are $19,500, including the recovery; do not deduct that recovery twice.')+p('Opening net AR is $60,000 − $1,200 = $58,800. Add $40,000 credit sales, deduct $19,500 cash collections and $2,100 provision to get $77,200 closing net AR. A write-off against an existing allowance reduces gross AR and allowance together, so it does not by itself create a second net-AR loss in this model.')),
('review','Keep approval, evidence and estimation separate',p('Support write-offs and recoveries with approved customer records. Reconcile the detailed customer ledger to the gross AR control, and the allowance schedule to its own control account. A customer promise is not collected cash; a write-off is not a cash payment. Document the estimate owner, review date and changes in assumptions.')+p('Use '+link('accounts-receivable-process','the receivable workflow')+' for invoice detail and '+link('bad-debt-expense','the bad-debt expense guide')+' for the event-by-event provision, write-off and recovery entries. The '+link('current-assets','current-assets guide')+' explains gross versus net presentation. Tax deductibility is a separate determination and is not established by an allowance entry.'))
],['Movement / target','Amount'],allowroll,['Bucket / cutoff / evidence','Gross exposure','Supported loss assumption','Required allowance','Existing balance / provision / reviewer'],[('Is the required allowance the same as this period’s expense?','Not necessarily. Reconcile the existing debit or credit balance and intervening movements first.'),('Are the 1%, 4% and 15% rates recommended?','No. They are fictional inputs to demonstrate arithmetic, not a compliant estimate for another business.'),('Does writing off an already provided balance create another expense?','Under this allowance example, it reduces gross AR and allowance together rather than recognizing the expense twice.'),('Does the guide apply every current US GAAP simplification?','No. The case assumes neither ASU 2025-05 election; eligibility, consistency and disclosures require separate review.')],[(FASB,'FASB ASU 2025-05: current receivables measurement options'),('https://www.fasb.org/page/PageContent?pageId=%2Fprojects%2Fother-staff-projects%2Ffasb-staff-qatopic-326-no-2developing-an-estimate-of-expect.html','FASB staff Q&A: estimating expected credit losses'),(LOSS,'OpenStax: allowance-account mechanics; not a complete current CECL model')],'Credit analyst reviewing aging folders; titled Allowance for Doubtful Accounts.')

badrows=[['Opening balances','25,000','1,500 credit','23,500','0'],['Record 900 provision','25,000','2,400 credit','22,600','0'],['Write off 1,200','23,800','1,200 credit','22,600','0'],['Reinstate recovered 300','24,100','1,500 credit','22,600','0'],['Collect recovered 300','23,800','1,500 credit','22,300','+300']]
add('N036','bad-debt-expense','Bad Debt Expense: Provision, Write-Off and Recovery','Bad Debt Expense: Write-Off and Recovery','Separate bad-debt expense recognition, an allowance write-off and a later recovery with balanced entries and a reconciled gross, allowance and net-AR example.','bad debt expense','Event-level accounting mechanics; allowance estimation method and tax deductibility remain separate.','Bad debt expense recognizes a credit-loss provision under the selected accounting policy. A later write-off and recovery are different events. Under an allowance approach, do not record the same expected loss again as expense when the customer balance is removed.',[
('scope','Identify whether you are estimating, writing off or collecting',p('This original example uses a US GAAP-style allowance approach for trade receivables, solely to explain posting mechanics. It does not build the full expected-credit-loss estimate. Opening gross AR is $25,000 and allowance is $1,500 credit. Assume a separately supported review requires a $900 current provision before the later events below; there are no other sales, ordinary collections, credits or tax effects.')+p('The '+link('allowance-doubtful-accounts','allowance guide')+' owns estimation, including current FASB policy choices. A percentage of sales or a textbook aging table should not automatically be described as complete current US GAAP compliance. Keep the entity’s applicable method and approval evidence visible.')),
('entries','Record each event with its own balanced entry',table('Original allowance-method entries, USD',['Event','Debit','Credit','Cash at this step'],[['Provision','Credit loss / bad debt expense 900','Allowance 900','None'],['Approved write-off','Allowance 1,200','Customer receivable 1,200','None'],['Reinstate recovered balance','Customer receivable 300','Allowance 300','None'],['Collect recovered balance','Cash 300','Customer receivable 300','Receipt 300']])+p('The provision reduces current profit by $900 under this case’s expense convention. The write-off uses the existing allowance. Reinstatement restores the customer item before the supported receipt is allocated. Do not treat the recovered $300 as another sale or automatically reverse $300 of expense without examining the actual policy and allowance estimate.')+p('A write-off decision requires documented authorization and the relevant accounting assessment. It is not created merely by reaching an arbitrary number of days overdue. Collection rights, customer communications and tax treatment can involve separate considerations.')),
('balances','Follow gross AR, allowance and net AR through the sequence',table('Original event-level balance bridge; no other movements',['Stage','Gross AR','Allowance','Net AR','Cash movement'],badrows)+p('The $1,200 write-off reduces gross AR from $25,000 to $23,800 and allowance from $2,400 to $1,200, leaving net AR unchanged at <strong>$22,600</strong>. Reinstating $300 increases both balances and still leaves net AR at $22,600. Collecting $300 then changes the composition of assets: net AR becomes $22,300 and cash increases $300.')+p('Across the full sequence, opening net AR $23,500 − $900 provision − $300 recovered cash collection = $22,300. The write-off is not a $1,200 cash payment, and the recovery is not $300 new revenue. Reassess the closing allowance under the actual reporting policy after all relevant evidence is considered.')),
('contrast','Do not confuse an allowance provision with direct write-off treatment',p('A direct write-off presentation records an expense when a specific receivable is removed, rather than first using an allowance. That timing differs from the modeled sequence. It is not presented here as an unrestricted alternative for financial reporting or as automatic tax treatment. Method suitability, materiality, framework and current requirements need their own review.')+p('Financial-reporting expense, customer-ledger removal and tax deduction can occur under different conditions. Recording an allowance or approving a customer write-off does not itself prove a tax deduction is available. This guide intentionally makes no deduction amount, eligibility or filing claim.')),
('controls','Reconcile and preserve the recovery trail',p('Retain customer ID, original invoice, provision policy, write-off approval, reinstatement reference, bank evidence and cash allocation. Check the customer subledger against gross AR and the allowance rollforward against its control account at the same cutoff. If a receipt cannot be identified, investigate rather than attaching it to a convenient written-off balance.')+p('Use '+link('accounts-receivable-process','the collection workflow')+' for payment matching, '+link('general-ledger-template','the general ledger record')+' for posting references and '+link('accounting-cycle','the accounting cycle')+' for the reviewed close. A clear audit trail keeps estimation uncertainty separate from an observed bank receipt.'))
],['Stage','Gross AR','Allowance','Net AR','Cash movement'],badrows,['Event / customer / reference','Debit account / amount','Credit account / amount','Gross / allowance / net effect','Cash / approval / reviewer'],[('Does a write-off always create new bad debt expense?','Not under this allowance example. It uses a balance that was already provided.'),('Is a recovered debt new sales revenue?','Not simply because cash arrived. Trace the original receivable and the applicable recovery treatment.'),('Is a write-off a cash outflow?','No. Removing the receivable does not itself move cash.'),('Does an accounting expense prove tax deductibility?','No. Tax conditions and financial-reporting entries require separate analysis.')],[(LOSS,'OpenStax: allowance write-off and recovery mechanics'),('https://finance.cornell.edu/accounting/topics/revenueclass/baddebt','Cornell: institutional illustration of expense versus allowance write-off; local policy is not universal')],'Administrator matching recovery evidence to a closed customer file; titled Bad Debt Expense.')

(R/'src/content/blogs/twelveDayDay4.js').write_text('export const twelveDayDay4Posts = '+json.dumps(posts,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+';\n')
(R/'docs/day4-release/cases.json').write_text(json.dumps([dict(slug=p['slug'],id=p['id'],keyword=p['primaryKeyword'],scope=p['scopeBoundary'])for p in posts],indent=2))
print('Prepared',len(posts),'original guides and editable worksheets')
